# ================================
# Project Structure Rules
# ================================
- Root contains exactly two folders:
  - /server → ASP.NET Core API (.NET 10), multi-project backend solution.
    - ASP.NET Core API should use Contoller
  - /client → React 18+ with TypeScript & TSX (Vite or Create React App).
- Never place code outside these folders.
- /server must contain a solution (ReactifyBlog.sln) with three projects:
  - ReactifyBlog.Api → ASP.NET Core minimal API (presentation layer).
  - ReactifyBlog.Business → Class library (business logic, services, DTOs, validations).
  - ReactifyBlog.Data → Class library (EF Core, repositories, entities, migrations).
- /server/Tests must contain test projects:
  - ReactifyBlog.Api.Tests
  - ReactifyBlog.Business.Tests
  - ReactifyBlog.Data.Tests
  - All tests use xUnit + Moq + FluentAssertions.

# ================================
# Backend Layering & Architecture Rules
# ================================
- Strict 3-layer separation:
  - ReactifyBlog.Api: Only HTTP concerns (controllers, middleware, DI setup).
  - ReactifyBlog.Business: Pure logic — services, validators, custom exceptions, constants.
  - ReactifyBlog.Data: Only data access — DbContext, repositories, EF configs.
- No cross-layer dependencies except downward:
  - Api → Business → Data
  - Business must never reference Api.
  - Data must never reference Business or Api.
- All projects use explicit namespace matching folder structure.
- All **EF Core entity models** (inside `/server/ReactifyBlog.Data/Models/`) **must end with `DBO` suffix** (e.g., `UserDBO`, `PostDBO`, `RefreshTokenDBO`).  
  → This enforces clear domain-boundary: Data layer owns raw entities; Business layer owns DTOs/Models.
- ✅ NO hard-coded strings anywhere in backend — all literals moved to constant classes in `ReactifyBlog.Business/Constants/`:
  • `DatabaseConstants.cs`:
      public static class DatabaseConstants
      {
          public const string DefaultConnectionStringName = "DefaultConnection";
      }
  • `CookieConstants.cs`:
      public static class CookieConstants
      {
          public const string IdentityCookieName = ".AspNetCore.Identity.ReactifyBlog";
          public const string RefreshTokenCookieName = "refresh_token";
      }
  • `SwaggerConstants.cs`:
      public static class SwaggerConstants
      {
          public const string ApiTitle = "ReactifyBlog API";
          public const string ApiVersion = "v1";
          public const string ApiDocName = "v1";
      }
  • `RouteConstants.cs` (optional but recommended):
      public static class RouteConstants
      {
          public const string ApiPrefix = "api";
          public static class Auth { public const string Base = "auth"; }
      }
-✅ All configuration in `Program.cs` must be extracted into extension methods:
  • Create folder: `/server/ReactifyBlog.Api/Extensions/`
  • File: `ServiceCollectionExtensions.cs`
      public static class ServiceCollectionExtensions
      {
          public static IServiceCollection AddDatabase(this IServiceCollection services, IConfiguration configuration)
          {
              services.AddDbContext<ReactifyBlogDbContext>(options =>
                  options.UseSqlServer(configuration.GetConnectionString(DatabaseConstants.DefaultConnectionStringName)));
              return services;
          }
          public static IServiceCollection AddApplicationCookie(this IServiceCollection services)
          {
              services.ConfigureApplicationCookie(options =>
              {
                  options.Cookie.Name = CookieConstants.IdentityCookieName;
                  options.Cookie.HttpOnly = true;
                  options.Cookie.SameSite = SameSiteMode.Strict;
                  options.Cookie.SecurePolicy = CookieSecurePolicy.Always;
                  options.ExpireTimeSpan = TimeSpan.FromMinutes(20);
                  options.SlidingExpiration = true;
              });
              return services;
          }
          public static IServiceCollection AddSwagger(this IServiceCollection services)
          {
              services.AddSwaggerGen(options =>
              {
                  options.SwaggerDoc(SwaggerConstants.ApiDocName, new OpenApiInfo
                  {
                      Title = SwaggerConstants.ApiTitle,
                      Version = SwaggerConstants.ApiVersion
                  });
              });
              return services;  
          }
      }
  • File: `ApplicationBuilderExtensions.cs`
      public static class ApplicationBuilderExtensions
      {
          public static IApplicationBuilder UseCustomExceptionHandling(this IApplicationBuilder app)
          {
              return app.UseMiddleware<ResponseHandlingMiddleware>();
          }
      }
  • Then `Program.cs` becomes minimal:
      var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);
      builder.Services
          .AddDatabase(builder.Configuration)
          .AddApplicationCookie()
          .AddSwagger()
          .AddControllers(); // or AddEndpoints, etc.
      var app = builder.Build();
      if (app.Environment.IsDevelopment()) app.UseSwagger();
      app.UseCustomExceptionHandling().UseHttpsRedirection().UseRouting().UseAuthentication().UseAuthorization().MapControllers();
      app.Run();

- ✅ This applies to ALL string literals — connection strings, cookie names, route templates, claim types, error messages in exceptions (use constants + interpolation), etc.

# ================================
# Custom Exception Handling Rules
# ================================
- Define base exception:  
  ReactifyBlog.Business/Exceptions/ReactifyBlogException.cs  
  → Properties: ErrorCode (string), ServiceName (string), StatusCode (int), Details (optional dict).
- For each service (e.g., RegisterService):
  - Create matching error constants class:  
    ReactifyBlog.Business/Constants/ErrorConstants/Exceptions/RegisterServiceErrorConstants.cs  
    → public const readonly fields like:  
    public const string UserExistsErrorCode = "REG_001";
    public const string UserExistsErrorMessage = "User exists.";  
    public const string InvalidEmailErrorCode = "REG_002";
    public const string InvalidEmailErrorMessage = "Entered email exists in database.";

  - When throwing:  
    throw new ReactifyBlogException(RegisterServiceErrorConstants.UserExistsErrorCode, nameof(RegisterService), 400);
- Centralized exception middleware in Api/Middleware/ResponseHandlingMiddleware.cs:
  - Catches ReactifyBlogException → returns AppResponse with Error set + proper status code.
  - Catches unexpected exceptions → logs + returns 500 with generic safe message.

# ================================
# API Response & DTO Rules
# ================================
- Define generic response wrapper:  
  ReactifyBlog.Business/DTOs/AppResponse.cs<T> →  
  public class AppResponse<T>
  {
      public T? Data { get; set; }
      public ErrorInfo? Error { get; set; }
  }
  Where ErrorInfo has: Code, Message, Service, ValidationErrors (for FluentValidation).
- All endpoints return AppResponse<T> (never raw data or exceptions).
- If HTTP status is 2xx → Data is filled, Error = null.
- If HTTP status is 4xx/5xx → Error is filled (with code/message/service), Data = null.
- FluentValidation:
  - Validators live in Business/Validators/.
  - On validation failure → middleware converts ValidationException to AppResponse with:  
    { "Error": { "Code": "VALIDATION_FAILED", "ValidationErrors": { "Email": ["Invalid format"] } } }

# ================================
# Identity & Authentication Rules
# ================================
- Use **ASP.NET Core Identity** (Microsoft.AspNetCore.Identity.EntityFrameworkCore) as the single source of truth for auth.
- ReactifyBlogDbContext must inherit from IdentityDbContext<ReactifyBlogUser, ReactifyBlogRole, Guid>.
- Default Identity cookie (named ".AspNetCore.Identity.ReactifyBlog") is used for authentication — HttpOnly, SameSite=Strict, Secure.
- Custom refresh token support:
  - On successful login, generate a cryptographically random refresh token (128-bit).
  - Store it (hashed + salted) in a new table: RefreshTokens (UserId, TokenHash, ExpiresAt, RevokedAt).
  - Send raw refresh token ONLY via secure, HttpOnly cookie named "refresh_token".
  - /auth/refresh endpoint validates refresh_token cookie → issues new Identity session (via SignInManager.SignInAsync).
- All auth logic wrapped in services (e.g., AuthService) — never expose SignInManager/UserManager to controllers.
- Claims & roles managed via Identity — [Authorize] and policies used for endpoint protection.
- Password policies, lockout, email confirmation → configured via IdentityOptions.
- Migrations must include Identity + RefreshTokens tables.

# ================================
# Data Layer (EF Core) Rules
# ================================
- Use **EF Core 9** (latest stable for .NET 10).
- DbContext lives in ReactifyBlog.Data/Data/ReactifyBlogDbContext.cs.
- All queries must be async (ToListAsync, FirstOrDefaultAsync, etc.).
- Use DbSet<TEntity> only in repositories — never in services or controllers.
- Repositories implement interfaces (e.g., IUserRepository) defined in ReactifyBlog.Business.
- Configurations (OnModelCreating) must be split into IEntityTypeConfiguration<TEntity> classes in ReactifyBlog.Data/Configurations/.
- Migrations generated only via Package Manager Console:  
  Add-Migration <Name> -Project ReactifyBlog.Data -StartupProject ReactifyBlog.Api
- Never expose EF entities outside ReactifyBlog.Data; mapping to DTOs happens in ReactifyBlog.Business/Mappings/ (AutoMapper or manual).

# ================================
# Code Quality & Style Rules (Backend)
# ================================
- No hard-coded strings: all keys, messages, routes, claim names → ReactifyBlog.Business/Constants.
- Braces always on new line, even for 1-liners:
  if (x == 1)
  {
      return;
  }
- One blank line between all members (fields, props, methods) — improves readability.
- Async all the way: no .Result or .Wait().
- DI-friendly design: all dependencies injected via constructor; no new Service() in logic.
- Unit-testable:
  - Services depend on interfaces (IUserRepository, not UserRepository).
  - No static/logic in controllers.
  - Test projects mirror structure.

# ================================
# React Frontend (TSX) Rules
# ================================
- /client uses Vite + React 18 + TypeScript + TSX.
- Folder structure:
  - /src
    - /components → reusable (dumb) components (PascalCase.tsx).
    - /pages → route-level pages (e.g., BlogPage.tsx).
    - /services → API clients (authService.ts, blogService.ts).
    - /hooks → custom hooks (useAuth.ts, useApi.ts).
    - /types → shared TS interfaces (mirroring backend DTOs).
    - /utils → helpers (no business logic).
    - /assets, /styles
- Strict TypeScript:
  - noImplicitAny, strict: true.
  - All props typed; no any.
- API layer:
  - All calls go through apiClient.ts (Fetch wrapper).
  - Automatically handles cookies (credentials: 'include').
  - Never throws — always returns AppResponse<T> (even on network/API errors).

- Base API Response Handling (MUST mirror backend AppResponse<T> exactly):
  - Define shared response types in /src/types/api.ts:
    export interface ErrorInfo {
      Code: string;
      Message: string;
      Service?: string;
      ValidationErrors?: Record<string, string[]>;
    }

    export interface AppResponse<T = undefined> {
      Data?: T | null;
      Error?: ErrorInfo | null;
    }

  - Every API service method MUST return Promise<AppResponse<T>> — never raw T or throw.
  - apiClient.ts behavior:
    • On 2xx–3xx: parse JSON → { Data: T, Error: null } → return as-is.
    • On 4xx/5xx: parse → { Error: { Code, Message, Service, ValidationErrors? }, Data: null }.
      → ValidationErrors from FluentValidation are preserved as-is for form binding.
    • On network failure: return synthetic AppResponse with Code: "NETWORK_ERROR", etc.
  - Usage in components/services:
    const res = await authService.login(credentials);
    if (res.Error) {
      if (res.Error.ValidationErrors) {
        // Handle field-level errors (e.g., React Hook Form setError)
      } else {
        // Show generic error (e.g., toast)
      }
    } else {
      // res.Data is safe to use
    }

- State management: React Context + useReducer for auth; no Redux unless scale demands.
- No inline styles — CSS Modules or Tailwind (configurable).
- Error boundaries for pages.

# ================================
# Generic API Service Specification (Mandatory)
# ================================
- MUST implement a generic HTTP service in /src/services/apiClient.ts:
  - Name: `httpClient`
  - Purpose: Unified, type-safe, cookie-aware HTTP client for all backend calls.
  - All domain services (authService, blogService, etc.) MUST use this — no direct fetch/axios.

- HTTP Methods constants (define in /src/constants/httpMethods.ts):
  export const HTTP_METHODS = {
    GET: 'GET',
    POST: 'POST',
    PUT: 'PUT',
    PATCH: 'PATCH',
    DELETE: 'DELETE'
  } as const;
  export type HttpMethod = typeof HTTP_METHODS[keyof typeof HTTP_METHODS];

- `httpClient` implementation (in /src/services/apiClient.ts):
  interface HttpClientOptions<TRequest = undefined> {
    url: string;
    method: HttpMethod;
    body?: TRequest;
    signal?: AbortSignal;
  }

  export const httpClient = {
    request: async <TResponse, TRequest = undefined>(
      options: HttpClientOptions<TRequest>
    ): Promise<AppResponse<TResponse>> => {
      const { url, method, body, signal } = options;

      const config: RequestInit = {
        method,
        credentials: 'include',
        headers: {
          'Content-Type': 'application/json',
        },
        signal,
      };

      if (body !== undefined && method !== HTTP_METHODS.GET && method !== HTTP_METHODS.DELETE) {
        config.body = JSON.stringify(body);
      }

      try {
        const res = await fetch(url, config);
        let data: AppResponse<TResponse>;
        try {
          data = await res.json();
        } catch {
          // Fallback on invalid JSON (e.g., 502, HTML error page)
          data = {
            Data: null,
            Error: {
              Code: 'PARSE_ERROR',
              Message: 'Invalid server response.',
              Service: 'HttpClient'
            }
          };
        }
        // Ensure shape — defensive programming
        if (data.Data === undefined) data.Data = null;
        if (data.Error === undefined) data.Error = null;
        // NOTE: ValidationErrors from backend are preserved here (e.g., { "Email": [...] })
        return data;
      } catch (err) {
        // Network-level failure (offline, DNS, timeout, CORS)
        return {
          Data: null,
          Error: {
            Code: 'NETWORK_ERROR',
            Message: 'Unable to reach server. Please check your connection.',
            Service: 'HttpClient'
          }
        };
      }
    },

    get: <T>(url: string, signal?: AbortSignal) => 
      httpClient.request<T, undefined>({ url, method: HTTP_METHODS.GET, signal }),

    post: <T, R = undefined>(url: string, body?: R, signal?: AbortSignal) => 
      httpClient.request<T, R>({ url, method: HTTP_METHODS.POST, body, signal }),

    put: <T, R = undefined>(url: string, body?: R, signal?: AbortSignal) => 
      httpClient.request<T, R>({ url, method: HTTP_METHODS.PUT, body, signal }),

    patch: <T, R = undefined>(url: string, body?: R, signal?: AbortSignal) => 
      httpClient.request<T, R>({ url, method: HTTP_METHODS.PATCH, body, signal }),

    delete: <T>(url: string, signal?: AbortSignal) => 
      httpClient.request<T, undefined>({ url, method: HTTP_METHODS.DELETE, signal })
  };

- Domain services MUST use it like:
  import { LoginRequest, LoginResponse } from '@/types/auth';
  export const login = (req: LoginRequest) => 
    httpClient.post<LoginResponse, LoginRequest>('/api/auth/login', req);
  
  export const logout = () => 
    httpClient.post<void>('/api/auth/logout');
  
  export const refreshSession = () => 
    httpClient.post<void>('/api/auth/refresh');

- Critical requirements:
  • `credentials: 'include'` is non-negotiable — enables automatic cookie transmission (auth + refresh_token).
  • No manual header/token injection — Identity cookie + refresh_token cookie handle auth fully.
  • All domain services are thin wrappers over `httpClient.{method}`.
  • Never use raw `fetch()` or `axios()` elsewhere.

# ================================
# Build & Validation Enforcement (MANDATORY FOR CURSOR)
# ================================
After every change:
1. Run: dotnet build /server/ReactifyBlog.sln --configuration Release
2. Run: cd /client && npm run build
3. If any error → fix immediately and retry.
4. Proceed only when both builds succeed with 0 errors.
Goal: Project must always be in buildable, error-free state.